Prefix: : <https://finsight-tech.com/ontology/examples/typed-relationships#>

Ontology: <https://finsight-tech.com/ontology/examples/typed-relationships>


# ************************************************************************
# Typed Financial Relationships
# ************************************************************************
#
# Shows why financial relationships should be named according to their
# business meaning rather than collapsed into a generic relatedTo property.
# The example also introduces inverse relationships.
#


### Core concepts

Class: Security

Class: Identifier

Class: Listing

Class: TradingVenue

Class: SystemRecord

Class: Equity

Class: EquityOption


### Security identification

ObjectProperty: identifiedBy
    Domain: Security
    Range: Identifier


### Security listing

ObjectProperty: hasListing
    Domain: Security
    Range: Listing
    InverseOf: listedSecurity

ObjectProperty: listedSecurity
    Domain: Listing
    Range: Security


### Listing venue

ObjectProperty: admittedTo
    Domain: Listing
    Range: TradingVenue


### System representation

ObjectProperty: represents
    Domain: SystemRecord
    Range: Security


### Product reference

ObjectProperty: referencesUnderlier
    Domain: EquityOption
    Range: Equity


### Modelling point

# These relationships connect different kinds of concepts for different
# reasons. The property name is part of the financial meaning:
#
# identifiedBy        - identification
# hasListing          - market presence
# admittedTo          - venue association
# represents          - information-system representation
# referencesUnderlier - economic reference
#
# A generic relatedTo property would hide these distinctions.
